Neglected Scrotal Mass (Delayed Presentation of Testicular Malignancy)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Painless hard testicular mass, Failure to transilluminate, Elevated serum AFP/beta-hCG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-024
Reason for Visit: Acute, unremitting back pain and new-onset breathlessness in a severely cachectic patient, requiring urgent evaluation for malignant complications.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated new-onset shortness of breath and severe, unremitting back pain in a cachectic patient with a previously undisclosed, rock-hard testicular mass. Urgent assessment was mandated to rule out acute pulmonary embolism or malignant spinal cord compression secondary to advanced metastatic disease, an evaluation that could not be delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My lower back is in absolute agony, I cannot catch my breath, and I have been ignoring a huge, hard lump in my scrotum for months because I was terrified.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing, deliberately ignored painless, rock-hard, heavily indurated unilateral testicular mass present for over eight months. They report a progressive dull ache and sensation of extreme heaviness in the lower abdomen and scrotum. Tonight, they developed acute, severe, unremitting back pain and sudden dyspnoea. They report a chronic cough indicating likely advanced pulmonary metastases. They have suffered unintentional profound weight loss and cachexia over the last four months. They also note recent breast tenderness and swelling (gynaecomastia).
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound tonight due to severe back pain and respiratory distress on exertion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme psychological denial regarding the illness; severe risk of acute respiratory or neurological collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.2C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Cachectic, pale, appearing chronically severely ill, demonstrating respiratory distress and extreme discomfort from back pain.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: A massive, painless, rock-hard unilateral testicular mass is present. The palpable mass is intimately inseparable from the testicle itself. There is a complete failure of the mass to transilluminate upon examination. Abdomen: Deep palpation reveals a firm, fixed, bulky mass in the mid-abdomen consistent with massive retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy. Respiratory: Decreased breath sounds at the right lung base. Lymphatic: A palpable firm supraclavicular lymph node (Virchow's node) is present on the left. Expected investigation results would include marked elevation of serum tumour markers (AFP and beta-hCG) alongside a solid hypervascular intratesticular mass on ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Patient has been living in squalor, heavily isolated due to the shame and fear of the neglected mass.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications; has avoided all medical contact.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Neglected Scrotal Mass (Delayed Presentation of Testicular Malignancy) - Status: Exacerbated (Metastatic Complications)
Differentials:

Hydrocele - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mass being rock-hard, completely failing to transilluminate, and being inextricably bound to the testis.

Epididymitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely painless, chronic nature of the mass and the profound presence of advanced cachexia and metastatic signs.

Inguinal Hernia - Ruled out/unlikely as the mass is distinctly testicular in origin, with no extension into the inguinal canal, alongside massive retroperitoneal disease.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient presents in extremis with the catastrophic sequelae of a neglected testicular malignancy. The presence of a rock-hard testicular mass combined with acute respiratory distress and severe back pain strongly indicates massive retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy and pulmonary metastases. The acute dyspnoea and tachycardia raise the immediate specter of an acute pulmonary embolism or malignant airway compromise. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely critical to identify these life-threatening systemic complications and initiate immediate emergency hospital transfer for life-saving oncological intervention. Delaying care would have predictably resulted in fatal respiratory or neurological collapse.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Arranged immediate priority ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary hospital Emergency Department for emergent CT staging (Chest/Abdomen/Pelvis) and urgent oncology/urology consultation. Administered Oxygen at 4L/min via nasal prongs. Administered SC Morphine 5mg for the severe metastatic back pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: No behavioral interventions are appropriate in this acute, life-threatening oncological emergency; immediate mechanical and medical stabilization is the sole priority.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Direct medical handover provided to the receiving Emergency Physician, emphasizing the delayed presentation and respiratory distress.
DME/Safety (Optional): None, requires immediate admission.
Follow-up: GP to liaise directly with the hospital oncology team regarding prognosis and eventual discharge planning.

